<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/maps-projections-and-gis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Maps are arguments: <strong>every projection distorts something, and every selection of
    layers emphasises a story</strong>. Modern geography's tool is the GIS — a system that stacks
    spatial data in layers and queries it, turning "where" into the most powerful question in
    public health, logistics, climate planning and urban design.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="projections, distortions and the birth of spatial analysis" widget="equationStep" config={projCfg} />

  <h2>The modern geographer's toolkit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Coordinate systems:</strong> latitude/longitude (angular), UTM (metre-precise zones). Get the datum wrong and your data lands in the wrong city — a real and expensive class of error.</li>
    <li><strong>Remote sensing feeds the layers:</strong> satellite imagery (Landsat since 1972 — the longest Earth record), radar, LiDAR. Deforestation, glacier retreat and urban sprawl are now measured, not estimated.</li>
    <li><strong>Spatial statistics:</strong> clusters, hot spots and correlations have their own tests (is that cancer cluster real, or random?). Geography's contribution to the statistics ladder.</li>
    <li><strong>Cartographic ethics:</strong> redistricting gerrymanders, "holes" in coverage, colour choices that exaggerate — maps persuade. Snow's cholera map, redistricting maps and election maps all prove it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Monmonier, How to Lie with Maps; Johnson, The Ghost Map (Snow, 1854); Esri & QGIS documentation.</p>
</TopicPage>
